From 9100f1c9bfd87873a91439ab75085d4cc75b30af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bmatusiak <bmatusiak@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 19:25:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] okcore: wiping an RSA slot clears its key type

OKWIPEPRIV for RSA1-RSA4 zeroed the slot's flash and answered
"Successfully wiped RSA Private Key", but left the slot's key type in
EEPROM. The next read still found a type, so okcore_flashget_RSA() ran
the AES-GCM decryption over the zeroed flash (no tag check: the result is
the slot's keystream), took that as p and q, and published their product
as the slot's public key. A wiped RSA slot never read as empty: it
answered OKGETPUBKEY with a modulus of the old key's size that is no key.

The ECC wipe already clears its type (wipe_private() zeroes the type byte
and ecc_priv_flash() stores it). The RSA wipe now does the same, so a
wiped RSA slot answers "Error no RSA Private Key set in this slot".
---
 onlykey/okcore.cpp | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/onlykey/okcore.cpp b/onlykey/okcore.cpp
index 0620dbc..db74456 100644
--- a/onlykey/okcore.cpp
+++ b/onlykey/okcore.cpp
@@ -5052,6 +5052,12 @@ void rsa_priv_flash(uint8_t *buffer, bool wipe) {
         flash_modify(buffer[5], temp, buffer, MAX_RSA_KEY_SIZE, 1);
         //Write buffer to flash
         okcore_flashset_common(tptr, (unsigned long *)adr, 2048);
+        /* The key is gone, so clear its type as well, as an ECC wipe does
+         * (ecc_priv_flash stores the zeroed type byte). Left set, the slot
+         * still reads as an RSA key: okcore_flashget_RSA decrypts the zeroed
+         * flash into keystream and rsa_getpub answers its product. */
+        uint8_t no_key = 0;
+        okeeprom_eeset_rsakey(&no_key, (int)buffer[5]);
         hidprint("Successfully wiped RSA Private Key");
         blink(2);
         return;
-- 
2.55.0

